<%-
  _rlib   = context.respond_to?('r_libpath') ? context.r_libpath.to_s : ''
  _course = _rlib.sub(%r{/R/.*\z}, '')
  _tools  = _course.empty? ? '' : "#{_course}/job-tools"
  _image  = "/shared/apptainerImages/#{context.imagefile}"
-%>
# Running R batch jobs

This folder contains the course's supported tools for running R scripts as Slurm
batch jobs.

The wrapper runs your script in the same Apptainer image used by the course
RStudio app. That means your batch job uses the same version of R and can use
the same course package library as your interactive RStudio session.

## Submit an R script

Work in a directory you own. Do not edit the files in this course folder.

Copy the wrapper:

```bash
cp <%= _tools %>/run-r-job.sh .
```

Then submit an R script:

```bash
sbatch run-r-job.sh my_script.R
```

For example, if your script is named `hw3-bootstrap.R`:

```bash
sbatch run-r-job.sh hw3-bootstrap.R
```

## Request different resources

The wrapper includes default Slurm settings. You can override them when you
submit the job.

For example, request four CPUs, a two-hour time limit, and the job name `hw3`:

```bash
sbatch -c 4 -t 02:00:00 -J hw3 run-r-job.sh hw3-bootstrap.R
```

Put Slurm options **before** the wrapper name:

```bash
# Correct
sbatch -c 4 -t 02:00:00 run-r-job.sh my_script.R
```

```bash
# Incorrect: these are passed to the wrapper instead of Slurm
sbatch run-r-job.sh my_script.R -c 4 -t 02:00:00
```

## Pass arguments to your R script

Anything after the R script name is passed to that script.

For example:

```bash
sbatch run-r-job.sh analysis.R input-data.csv 10000
```

In R, retrieve command-line arguments with:

```r
args <- commandArgs(trailingOnly = TRUE)
```

## Check your job

Show your jobs:

```bash
squeue -u "$USER"
```

Show completed-job information:

```bash
sacct -u "$USER"
```

Cancel a job:

```bash
scancel <job-id>
```

The wrapper writes output to a file named:

```text
slurm-<job-id>.out
```

in the directory from which you submitted the job. Submit from a directory you
own: this course folder is read-only to you, so a job started from inside it
cannot write its output.

## Do not edit the shared files

This folder contains the maintained course copies:

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `run-r-job.sh` | Runs an R script as a Slurm job in the course R environment. |
| `course-env.sh` | Defines the current image and course R-library settings. |
| `README.md` | This file. |

Copy `run-r-job.sh` into your own working directory before submitting a job. Do not
edit `course-env.sh`.

The wrapper reads the current course settings when it runs, so a copy you made
earlier will continue to use the current course image and package library.
